## 2. FAT32 vs NTFS vs ext4

| Feature | FAT32 | NTFS | ext4 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Max file size | 4 GB | 16 TB | 16 TB |
| Journaling | No | Yes | Yes |
| Permissions | None | ACLs | Unix perms |
| Inode-like structure | None (FAT table) | MFT | True inodes |
| Best for | USB/SD cards | Windows drives | Linux drives |

**Key conceptual point:** FAT32 has no inodes — it tracks clusters via the File Allocation Table, with filenames living in flat directory entries. NTFS uses an MFT record per file (closer to an inode). ext4 has genuine Unix-style inodes, separate from the directory entries naming them — which is why ext4 natively supports hard links and Unix permissions, while FAT32 can't do either.

